  "result": {
    "feasibility": {
      "verdict": "promising",
      "rationale": "No paper was found doing the exact 'train on Swiss Alps data, deploy in North American mountains' transfer for general terrain-weather prediction — this is a genuine literature gap. However, three adjacent precedents validate the core hypothesis: (1) Swiss-trained avalanche RF models transferred to the Pyrenees with ~60-70% zero-shot agreement (Bacardit et al. 2026), proving cross-range transfer works but degrades without local adaptation; (2) Sierra Nevada to Colorado Rockies SWE transfer learning improved R2 from 0.45 to 0.54-0.56 and reduced bias ~30% (El Halabi et al. 2025), confirming topographic predictors (elevation, slope, aspect) behave similarly enough across US mountain ranges for transfer to help; (3) the RainShift benchmark (Harder et al. 2026) rigorously demonstrates that geographic distribution shift degrades deep-learning downscaling models substantially, but explicit domain adaptation (quantile-mapping for zero-shot, fine-tuning for few-shot) recovers skill. The dominant 2024-2026 architecture pattern — LoRA/PEFT adapting a frozen foundation-model backbone with lightweight terrain-aware heads — makes the approach tractable without full retraining. Multiple open-weight pretrained models exist at relevant scales. The physics (mass conservation, terrain-forced flow) is well understood and constrainable. The gap is engineering integration and cross-continent validation, not fundamental science.",
      "precedent": "Sierra Nevada -> Colorado Rockies SWE transfer (El Halabi et al. 2025, JGR ML&C): R2 +20%, bias -30% with topographic predictors. Swiss Alps -> Pyrenees avalanche model transfer (Bacardit et al. 2026, Cold Regions Sci. Tech.): ~60-70% zero-shot agreement. RainShift benchmark (Harder et al. 2026): domain adaptation recovers downscaling skill across geographic regions. DEVINE wind downscaling CNN: trained on synthetic topographies, validated on real Alpine stations. granite-geospatial-wxc-downscaling: Prithvi WxC fine-tuned for 6x temperature / 8x wind downscaling with open weights."
    },
    "recommended_pretrain_data": [
      {
        "dataset": "PeakWeather (MeteoSwiss)",
        "why": "Purpose-built ML benchmark: 302 complex-terrain stations, 10-min resolution, 8+ years, ships with per-station topographic indices (elevation, slope, aspect, curvature, sky-view factor) specifically for spatiotemporal deep learning. Already packaged on HuggingFace. Start here for a working baseline before adding density from other sources.",
        "accessibility": "Open. GitHub: MeteoSwiss/PeakWeather, HuggingFace: MeteoSwiss/PeakWeather, arXiv:2506.13652",
        "priority": 1
      },
      {
        "dataset": "HRRR 3km CONUS (NOAA) — already in use",
        "why": "The project already ingests HRRR. StormCast was trained on this exact data at 3km with dense boundary-layer vertical resolution. Using HRRR as the coarse-input conditioning signal for a terrain downscaling head means zero new data pipeline work for the backbone stage, and StormCast provides a pre-learned representation of HRRR physics.",
        "accessibility": "Open. AWS S3: noaa-hrrr-bdp-pds bucket. Already integrated in the current system.",
        "priority": 2
      },
      {
        "dataset": "Swiss IMIS Network (SLF)",
        "why": "180-200 automatic high-alpine stations, mostly above treeline near avalanche starting zones (2000-3000m), 30-min interval. Live REST API (measurement-api.slf.ch) plus historical data on EnviDat (CC BY 4.0). The most avalanche-relevant dense station network in existence. Use to supplement PeakWeather with high-elevation avalanche-zone observations.",
        "accessibility": "Open. Live API: measurement-api.slf.ch/v1 (unauthenticated). Historical: envidat.ch/dataset/imis-measuring-network (DOI 10.16904/envidat.406)",
        "priority": 3
      },
      {
        "dataset": "Reynolds Creek Experimental Watershed gridded product",
        "why": "21TB hourly gridded product on a 10m LiDAR DEM using detrended kriging from 35 stations over 238 km2, 40-year record. The terrain-station-weather relationship is already spatially continuous and explicit — ideal for learning terrain-driven downscaling physics as supervised training pairs (coarse input -> gridded fine output over known terrain).",
        "accessibility": "Open. ESSD doi 10.5194/essd-10-1197-2018. HydroShare RCCZO resources.",
        "priority": 4
      },
      {
        "dataset": "Synthetic CFD terrain-wind data (HuggingFace wind-cfd-trial + WindNinja-generated)",
        "why": "wind-cfd-trial provides ~10,000 RANS CFD simulation cases over ~1,000 real-world complex-terrain locations at 30m resolution, explicitly built for training ML terrain-wind models. Supplement with locally-generated WindNinja runs over Colorado avalanche-path DEMs to create target-domain synthetic training pairs. This is the proven DEVINE/FuXi-CFD pretraining strategy — learn terrain physics from physics simulators, not sparse stations.",
        "accessibility": "Open. HuggingFace: souravsud/wind-cfd-trial. WindNinja: public domain, pip install windninjapy.",
        "priority": 5
      },
      {
        "dataset": "SAFRAN-SURFEX/Crocus S2M Reanalysis (French Alps/Pyrenees)",
        "why": "Massif-resolved, terrain-aware (elevation/slope/aspect) meteorological and snow-cover reanalysis, 1958-2021. Already encodes hand-built terrain-weather physics stratified by terrain attributes. Use as physically-consistent training labels or as a benchmark to validate whether a learned model recovers the same terrain-weather relationships that operational meteorologists encoded manually.",
        "accessibility": "Open. ESSD doi 10.5194/essd-14-1707-2022. cnrm.sedoo.fr/s2m/",
        "priority": 6
      },
      {
        "dataset": "SNOTEL + CAIC multi-operator aggregator (Colorado target domain)",
        "why": "118 SNOTEL stations in current system plus CAIC's Jan 2026 aggregator (ski areas + CDOT + CAIC own network, hundreds of stations). This is the fine-tuning target, not pretraining data. Compute nearest-neighbor distances to find sub-5km clusters (likely along I-70 corridor and around major ski areas) for local validation.",
        "accessibility": "Open. SNOTEL: AWDB REST API. CAIC: stations.avalanche.state.co.us/zones.php",
