      {
        "dataset": "WFIP2 Columbia Gorge + WindNinja field validation campaigns",
        "why": "Dense (sub-km) multi-instrument arrays purpose-built to resolve terrain-induced flow: gap winds, mountain waves, wakes. Use for physics-validation testing — if the model cannot reproduce documented terrain-flow phenomena from these campaigns, it has not learned real terrain physics. Not for training (too short), but essential for evaluation.",
        "accessibility": "Open. WFIP2: AMS BAMS 2019 / OSTI 1409835. WindNinja: ninjastorm.firelab.org/windninja/field/",
        "priority": 8
      }
    ],
    "recommended_architecture": {
      "name": "StormCast backbone + DEVINE-style terrain downscaling head with LoRA adaptation",
      "description": "Two-stage architecture: (1) NVIDIA StormCast as the mesoscale backbone — the only open-weight model trained at actual terrain/mesoscale resolution (3km HRRR), with dense boundary-layer vertical resolution, 99 state variables, and a commercially-permissive license (NVIDIA Open Model License). It already understands HRRR-scale dynamics over CONUS. (2) A terrain-aware downscaling head inspired by DEVINE's U-Net architecture — takes StormCast's 3km output plus high-resolution DEM-derived terrain features (elevation, slope, aspect, curvature, Sx wind-exposure index, roughness/vegetation class) and produces sub-km wind, temperature, and precipitation fields at specific terrain locations. Mass conservation enforced as a hard architectural constraint on the wind output (divergence-free projection layer), following the JMLR hard-constrained downscaling pattern that demonstrably improves out-of-distribution generalization. The terrain encoder uses the proven minimal feature set recurring across WindNinja, WAsP, DEVINE, and PeakWeather: elevation, slope, aspect, curvature, sky-view factor, Sx upwind exposure, and roughness class — all computable from a standard DEM plus land-cover data.",
      "pretrain_approach": "Phase 1 — Synthetic terrain physics pretraining: Train the downscaling head on synthetic CFD data (HuggingFace wind-cfd-trial 10K cases + locally-generated WindNinja runs over diverse terrain) to learn general terrain-flow physics without needing dense real stations. This mirrors the proven DEVINE strategy (trained on 7,279 synthetic Gaussian topographies) and FuXi-CFD (trained on large CFD corpus). Phase 2 — Dense Alpine station training: Fine-tune on PeakWeather (302 Swiss stations, 10-min, 8+ years with topographic indices) plus Swiss IMIS high-alpine data (~180 stations), learning the statistical relationship between terrain descriptors and observed weather across a dense mountain network. Phase 3 — Reynolds Creek gridded product: Further train on the 21TB hourly 10m-grid product to learn terrain-resolved spatial patterns from a single well-instrumented catchment. Throughout, pair StormCast or ERA5 as the coarse atmospheric conditioning input.",
      "finetune_approach": "LoRA/PEFT adaptation of the frozen StormCast backbone for the Colorado domain (following the WeatherPEFT and MENA regional adaptation patterns — arXiv 2509.22020, 2409.07585). Fine-tune the terrain downscaling head on SNOTEL + CAIC aggregated Colorado stations using few-shot domain adaptation per the RainShift protocol: (1) quantile-mapping data alignment between Alpine and Rockies climate distributions for zero-shot baseline, (2) few-shot fine-tuning on the densest Colorado station clusters (I-70 corridor, ski areas where sub-5km spacing exists), (3) evaluate on held-out Colorado stations. Expected baseline: ~60-70% skill retention from zero-shot transfer (per Alps-to-Pyrenees precedent), improving to ~80-85% with few-shot fine-tuning (per Sierra-Nevada-to-Rockies SWE precedent of +20% R2 gain). Add mass conservation loss term during fine-tuning to improve out-of-distribution generalization to unseen Colorado terrain.",
      "precedent": "StormCast: trained on HRRR 3km, open weights on HuggingFace (nvidia/stormcast-v1-era5-hrrr), NVIDIA Open Model License. DEVINE: U-Net wind downscaling to 30m over complex terrain, pretrained weights on Zenodo, purpose-built for snow-transport/avalanche applications. WeatherPEFT (ICLR 2026): LoRA adapters on weather foundation models for regional specialization. Global-to-Local framework (arXiv 2607.03279): lightweight prediction heads on frozen backbone achieve ~100x resolution increase. Hard-Constrained Deep Learning for Climate Downscaling (JMLR 24(365)): architectural conservation constraints improve OOD generalization."
    },
    "existing_models": [
      {
        "name": "NVIDIA StormCast",
        "what_it_does": "Autoregressively predicts 99 state variables at 3km resolution over CONUS, trained on HRRR data with dense boundary-layer vertical resolution and 1-hour timestep. Diffusion-based mesoscale model.",
        "how_we_use_it": "Primary backbone. Already operates at near-terrain-scale (3km) over CONUS including Colorado. Freeze most parameters, apply LoRA adapters for Colorado-specific fine-tuning, and attach a terrain downscaling head for sub-km output. Its HRRR training means it already understands the same data our system ingests.",
