      },
      {
        "name": "WindNinja",
        "what_it_does": "Open-source (public domain) terrain wind-flow model with mass-conserving and RANS CFD solvers, widely used operationally for wildfire, wind energy, and search-and-rescue. Python bindings available (windninjapy).",
        "how_we_use_it": "Not a neural model — use as (a) a synthetic training-data generator: run WindNinja over Colorado avalanche-path DEMs to create terrain-to-wind-field training pairs, (b) a physics baseline to benchmark our learned model against, and (c) a reference for the proven minimal terrain-feature schema (DEM + vegetation/roughness + initial wind).",
        "source": "github.com/firelab/windninja, pip: windninjapy",
        "pretrained_weights": false
      }
    ],
    "novel_approach": "Pair NASA/IBM Prithvi-EO (geospatial/land-cover foundation model trained on Harmonized Landsat/Sentinel-2 imagery) as a terrain encoder with a weather backbone (StormCast or AIFS), following arXiv 2608.12271 which demonstrated that earth-observation foundation-model embeddings used as sub-grid terrain/land-surface descriptors to condition a downscaling model outperform hand-engineered terrain features. This cross-domain pairing — a computer-vision model trained on satellite imagery providing the 'what does this terrain look like' representation, fused with an atmospheric model providing the 'what is the weather doing' representation — is the most creative architecture the research surfaced. It sidesteps the terrain-feature-engineering problem entirely: instead of manually computing slope, aspect, curvature, sky-view factor, and roughness from DEMs, let a pretrained vision model learn what terrain features matter for weather from raw satellite imagery. The Prithvi-EO weights are open (Apache-style), and the approach has been validated in at least one published downscaling study but never applied to avalanche terrain-weather prediction specifically.",
    "risks": [
      "Domain shift between European Alps and Colorado Rockies is real and quantified: the best available precedent (Alps-to-Pyrenees avalanche transfer) shows ~30-40% accuracy degradation from zero-shot cross-range transfer due to different snow climate regimes, terrain geometry scales, and continental vs. maritime influence patterns. Budget for explicit domain adaptation, not just hope.",
      "Station density in Colorado (~118 SNOTEL + CAIC aggregated stations) may be insufficient for meaningful fine-tuning — the dense Alpine networks have 180-300+ stations in much smaller areas. Compute actual nearest-neighbor distance histograms for Colorado stations before assuming sub-5km clusters exist; if they do not, fine-tuning signal will be weak.",
      "Orographic precipitation ML is significantly less mature than wind downscaling — the literature is still largely correlational/statistical (CNN downscaling, radar+DEM kernels) rather than physics-constrained. Wind and temperature transfer may work well while precipitation (critical for avalanche forecasting) lags behind.",
      "Safety-critical application risk: avalanche forecasting is life-safety. A model that appears to work on average but fails on the specific terrain configurations and weather patterns that produce dangerous avalanches (wind-loaded leeward slopes, temperature inversions in starting zones) could be worse than no model at all. Evaluation must focus on the dangerous tail, not average skill.",
      "License ambiguity on key models: Aurora weights are listed as 'Other' license requiring Microsoft contact for commercial use; GraphCast/GenCast weights are CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 (non-commercial only). If the terrain-weather API has any commercial angle, the usable model set narrows to StormCast, FourCastNet3, AIFS, and ClimaX. Confirm licenses before investing training compute.",
      "Synthetic CFD training data (DEVINE-style, WindNinja-generated) may not capture the full complexity of real atmospheric boundary-layer dynamics over terrain — CFD simplifies turbulence (RANS steady-state vs. real transient flow), omits moisture/precipitation entirely, and assumes idealized boundary conditions. Models pretrained on synthetic data alone will need real-observation fine-tuning.",
      "Computational cost of fine-tuning billion-parameter foundation models (Prithvi-WxC is 2.3B parameters, Aurora is comparable) may exceed project resources even with LoRA/PEFT. StormCast's model size should be evaluated against available GPU memory before committing.",
      "Validation paradox: the whole point is predicting weather where stations do not exist, but validation requires ground truth at those locations. Short-term field campaigns (like WFIP2 or T-REX) provide sub-km validation data but only for weeks, not climatological periods. Plan for leave-one-station-out cross-validation on existing networks as the primary evaluation, supplemented by physics-consistency checks (mass conservation, terrain-flow phenomenology).",
      "The hyperlocal weather API market ($4.35B by 2034) is attractive but monetization is explicitly flagged as harder than forecast quality (per WindBorne Systems coverage). Building a technically excellent terrain-weather model does not guarantee a viable business without a clear go-to-market for specific verticals."
    ],
    "action_plan": [
      {
        "step": 1,
        "description": "Download and evaluate existing pretrained models on Colorado data. (a) Download StormCast weights from HuggingFace (nvidia/stormcast-v1-era5-hrrr), run inference over Colorado HRRR domain for a recent winter storm period, compare 3km predictions against SNOTEL observations for wind, temperature, precipitation. (b) Download granite-geospatial-wxc-downscaling from HuggingFace, evaluate its 6x temperature and 8x wind downscaling against the same SNOTEL stations. (c) Download FuXi-CFD ONNX model (daisy1212/FuXi-CFD-model), run over 5-10 Colorado avalanche-path DEMs, qualitatively assess whether terrain-flow features (ridge acceleration, valley channeling, lee eddies) appear physically plausible. Establish baseline skill scores for each.",
        "effort": "2 weeks",
        "data_source": "HRRR (already in system), SNOTEL (already in system), StormCast/granite-wxc/FuXi-CFD weights from HuggingFace"
      },
      {
        "step": 2,
        "description": "Build the terrain feature encoder. Compute DEM-derived terrain descriptors for all SNOTEL station locations and a 30m grid over Colorado avalanche terrain: elevation, slope, aspect, plan/profile curvature, Sx upwind exposure index (at 8 cardinal directions), sky-view factor, topographic position index, and NLCD roughness/vegetation class. Use the same feature set documented in PeakWeather (MeteoSwiss) and DEVINE to ensure compatibility with pretrained models. Store as a static terrain feature tensor indexed by lat/lon.",
        "effort": "1 week",
        "data_source": "USGS 1/3 arc-second DEM (already available), NLCD land cover, PeakWeather topographic index definitions (GitHub MeteoSwiss/PeakWeather)"
      },
      {
        "step": 3,
        "description": "Download PeakWeather dataset and Swiss IMIS historical data. (a) Pull PeakWeather from HuggingFace (MeteoSwiss/PeakWeather): 302 stations, 10-min data, 8+ years, with topographic indices. (b) Pull IMIS historical data from EnviDat (DOI 10.16904/envidat.406) for the ~180 high-alpine stations. (c) Compute nearest-neighbor distance histograms for both networks to identify sub-5km clusters. (d) Also compute nearest-neighbor distances for SNOTEL + CAIC aggregated Colorado stations to quantify the density gap between source (Swiss) and target (Colorado) domains.",
        "effort": "1 week",
        "data_source": "HuggingFace MeteoSwiss/PeakWeather, EnviDat IMIS, SNOTEL AWDB API, CAIC stations.avalanche.state.co.us"
      },
      {
        "step": 4,
        "description": "Generate synthetic terrain-wind training data over Colorado avalanche terrain. (a) Install WindNinja (pip install windninjapy). (b) Run WindNinja's mass-conserving solver over 50-100 Colorado avalanche-path DEMs at 30m resolution for 16 wind directions x 5 wind speeds = 80 scenarios each, producing ~4,000-8,000 terrain-to-wind-field training pairs. (c) Download HuggingFace wind-cfd-trial dataset (~10,000 RANS CFD cases over ~1,000 real terrain locations). (d) Combine into a unified synthetic pretraining corpus with standardized terrain feature inputs and wind field outputs.",
        "effort": "2 weeks",
        "data_source": "WindNinja (public domain), HuggingFace souravsud/wind-cfd-trial, USGS DEMs for Colorado avalanche paths"
      },
      {
        "step": 5,
        "description": "Clone DEVINE and train the terrain downscaling head. (a) Clone github.com/louisletoumelin/wind_downscaling_cnn, inspect architecture and pretrained weights. (b) Extend the U-Net architecture from wind-only to multi-variable (wind + temperature + relative humidity) output, keeping the terrain encoder unchanged. (c) Pretrain on synthetic CFD data from Step 4 to learn general terrain-flow physics. (d) Fine-tune on PeakWeather + IMIS observations (Step 3) paired with ERA5 or HRRR-equivalent coarse input, learning the statistical terrain-weather relationship from dense Alpine stations. Add a hard mass-conservation constraint layer (divergence-free projection) on the wind output channels, following JMLR 24(365) Hard-Constrained Deep Learning pattern.",
        "effort": "4 weeks",
        "data_source": "Synthetic CFD corpus (Step 4), PeakWeather + IMIS (Step 3), ERA5 coarse fields"
      },
      {
        "step": 6,
        "description": "Integrate StormCast backbone with terrain downscaling head. (a) Apply LoRA adapters to the frozen StormCast backbone, fine-tuning only adapter parameters on Colorado HRRR data with SNOTEL station targets (following WeatherPEFT pattern, arXiv 2509.22020). (b) Connect StormCast's 3km output as input conditioning for the terrain downscaling head from Step 5. (c) Apply quantile-mapping domain alignment between Swiss Alpine and Colorado Rockies climate distributions (per RainShift protocol) before feeding Swiss-pretrained head weights into the Colorado pipeline. (d) Fine-tune the full pipeline end-to-end on SNOTEL + CAIC Colorado stations, using leave-one-station-out cross-validation for evaluation.",
        "effort": "4 weeks",
        "data_source": "StormCast weights (Step 1), terrain head weights (Step 5), SNOTEL + CAIC Colorado observations"
      },
      {
        "step": 7,
        "description": "Validate on held-out data and physics-consistency checks. (a) Leave-one-station-out cross-validation across all Colorado stations: compute RMSE, bias, and correlation for wind speed/direction, temperature, and precipitation at each held-out station. (b) Physics-consistency tests: verify mass conservation (divergence) of predicted wind fields, check that predicted temperature inversions match known cold-air-pooling terrain configurations, verify orographic precipitation enhancement ratios against PRISM climatology. (c) If WFIP2 Columbia Gorge data is accessible, evaluate whether the model reproduces documented gap-flow and mountain-wave phenomena as a terrain-physics-learning diagnostic. (d) Compare against baselines: raw HRRR, WindNinja physics solver, simple elevation-based lapse rate, and granite-wxc-downscaling.",
        "effort": "2 weeks",
        "data_source": "Held-out SNOTEL/CAIC stations, WFIP2 field campaign data, PRISM climatology"
      },
      {
        "step": 8,
        "description": "Deploy as Phase 2 micro-weather prediction module. (a) Integrate the trained two-stage model (StormCast backbone + terrain head) into the existing snowpack instability prediction service. (b) Generate predictions at arbitrary terrain locations (specific avalanche paths, ridgelines, valley floors) as inputs to the instability model, replacing or augmenting the current HRRR-only weather inputs. (c) Run parallel evaluation: compare instability predictions using old (HRRR-only) vs. new (terrain-downscaled) weather inputs against CAIC avalanche observations for one winter season. (d) Publish results — this would be among the first cross-range terrain-weather transfer learning evaluations and is publishable regardless of whether it improves operational skill.",
        "effort": "4 weeks for integration, 1 winter season for parallel evaluation",
        "data_source": "Trained model from Steps 5-6, existing snowpack instability service, CAIC avalanche observations"
      }
    ]
  },
